1024
00:53:01.290 --> 00:53:09.550
During World War II, there were a group of Polish prisoners who were taken to a Nazi death

1025
00:53:09.550 --> 00:53:17.110
camp, and they used their urine as invisible ink to write letters detailing the horrific

1026
00:53:17.110 --> 00:53:19.790
experiences that were going on at the camp.

1027
00:53:19.790 --> 00:53:25.790
They were a group of young Polish girls and women who had been part of the Polish underground

1028
00:53:25.790 --> 00:53:33.370
resistance to the Nazi regime, and they were captured, they were imprisoned, and this group

1029
00:53:33.370 --> 00:53:42.930
were called the Rabbits of Ravensbrück because, and this is horrible, they were subjected to

1030
00:53:42.930 --> 00:53:46.550
extreme medical experiments like rabbits.

1031
00:53:46.950 --> 00:53:48.750
That's why they got this nickname.

1032
00:53:48.750 --> 00:53:56.130
However, they were allowed to write letters to their families back home once a month, but

1033
00:53:56.130 --> 00:53:57.810
the letters were heavily censored.

1034
00:53:58.130 --> 00:54:02.990
One of these women remembered that when she was a child, her and her brother had read this

1035
00:54:02.990 --> 00:54:08.310
series of books where people were writing messages in invisible ink.

1036
00:54:08.310 --> 00:54:18.250
So, they used their urine to write these secret messages, and due to a reaction with the paper,

1037
00:54:18.630 --> 00:54:26.070
the urine loses its color and becomes invisible, but then if you heat it up, it reveals what was

1038
00:54:26.070 --> 00:54:26.370
written.

1039
00:54:27.410 --> 00:54:33.930
And in her first letter to her brother, she wrote about these books that they read as kids,

1040
00:54:33.930 --> 00:54:38.030
and he figured out the code and knew how to reveal the hidden messages.

1041
00:54:38.790 --> 00:54:45.630
So, they came up with this code with their families, which was, if the hidden messages were revealed,

1042
00:54:45.850 --> 00:54:51.690
the families would send back a letter with a little piece of blue string, and that's how they

1043
00:54:51.690 --> 00:54:53.950
knew if their messages were being received.

1044
00:54:53.950 --> 00:55:00.990
So, eventually, the contents of these letters and the horrific details of the camp were broadcast

1045
00:55:00.990 --> 00:55:07.210
on the radio, and the camp was liberated by the Red Army, and the four women who wrote the letters

1046
00:55:07.210 --> 00:55:08.250
all survived.

1047
00:55:08.710 --> 00:55:12.350
Their letters were used as evidence to prosecute the guards.

1048
00:55:12.350 --> 00:55:13.030
Wow.

1049
00:55:13.390 --> 00:55:21.850
And 77 years later, these letters can still be read because 27 of them are still readable and on display

1050
00:55:21.850 --> 00:55:24.190
in a museum in Lublin.

1051
00:55:24.430 --> 00:55:25.690
Oh, my gosh.

1052
00:55:25.950 --> 00:55:27.910
That is an amazing story.

1053
00:55:28.370 --> 00:55:29.010
Amazing.

1054
00:55:29.410 --> 00:55:31.090
I was completely riveted.

1055
00:55:31.510 --> 00:55:32.790
I couldn't believe it.

1056
00:55:33.130 --> 00:55:34.430
I couldn't believe it.

1057
00:55:34.850 --> 00:55:38.750
Yeah, I'm just sort of speechless at the gravity of that story and how it's amazing.

